---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const emailCanvas = "mailto:olga@okad.agency?subject=Canvas%20UGC%20campaign%20%E2%80%94%20tell%20us%20about%20your%20brand";
const calHref = "https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first";
---

<Layout
  title="Canvas UGC Examples — Real Campaigns, Formats & What Works | OKAD"
  description="Real canvas UGC campaign examples across beauty, tech, food, fitness, and fashion. See the formats, creator structures, and metrics behind campaigns that generate millions of organic views."
>
  <Header />
  <main>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="pt-20 md:pt-24 pb-10 text-center bg-bg">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[12px] font-medium text-accent bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.18)] rounded-full px-3.5 py-1.5 mb-6">
          Canvas UGC &middot; Examples
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(28px,5vw,48px)] leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink mb-4">
          Canvas UGC Examples: <span class="text-accent">Real Campaigns That Work</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-[18px] text-ink-2 max-w-[600px] mx-auto leading-[1.6] mb-8">
          What does a canvas UGC program actually look like in practice? Five real campaign breakdowns covering structure, formats, creator counts, posting cadence, and the numbers behind them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-3 justify-center flex-wrap mb-8">
          <a href={emailCanvas} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-accent text-white hover:bg-accent-hover transition-all no-underline">Launch your canvas UGC campaign &rarr;</a>
          <a href={calHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-card text-ink border border-border hover:border-accent hover:text-accent transition-all no-underline">Book a call &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-5 flex-wrap justify-center font-mono text-[12px] text-muted">
          <span>5 real examples</span>
          <span>Beauty / Tech / Food / Fitness / Fashion</span>
          <span>Formats &amp; metrics</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <section class="py-10 border-t border-b border-border">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-muted-2 mb-4">Contents</div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-1.5">
          {[
            { num: "01", text: "What a Canvas UGC Campaign Looks Like", href: "#overview" },
            { num: "02", text: "Example: Beauty Brand on TikTok", href: "#beauty" },
            { num: "03", text: "Example: Tech/SaaS Product Demos", href: "#tech" },
            { num: "04", text: "Example: Food/CPG Recipe Content", href: "#food" },
            { num: "05", text: "Example: Fitness App Transformations", href: "#fitness" },
            { num: "06", text: "Example: Fashion Try-On Hauls", href: "#fashion" },
            { num: "07", text: "Content Formats That Perform Best", href: "#formats" },
            { num: "08", text: "How to Brief Creators", href: "#briefing" },
            { num: "09", text: "Metrics to Track", href: "#metrics" },
            { num: "10", text: "FAQ", href: "#faq" },
          ].map((item) => (
            <a href={item.href} class="flex items-center gap-2.5 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-[14px] text-ink-2 font-medium hover:bg-bg-alt transition-colors no-underline">
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium text-accent min-w-[22px]">{item.num}</span>
              {item.text}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 01 Overview -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="overview">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">01 &mdash; Overview</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">What a canvas UGC campaign looks like</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Brand creates niched social accounts and recruits a team of creators</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Creators post directly on brand accounts, paid per view (CPM $2&ndash;$6)</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Top organic posts get promoted as paid ads (Spark Ads, Partnership Ads)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A <a href="/canvas-ugc/" class="text-accent hover:underline">canvas UGC</a> campaign is not a single video or a one-off creator collab. It is an ongoing production system where multiple creators post content on brand-owned social accounts at volume. The brand's account is the "canvas" &mdash; the surface where all content lives and the audience accumulates.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Every successful canvas UGC campaign shares a few structural elements: a clearly niched account, a warmup period before scaling, 10+ creators posting consistently, a CPM-based payment structure that aligns creator incentives with performance, and a pipeline for graduating top organic performers into paid ads.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The examples below cover five different industries. Each one is based on real campaign structures &mdash; the kind of programs that agencies like OKAD build and operate for brands. The numbers are representative of what well-run programs achieve within the first 60&ndash;90 days.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 Beauty -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="beauty">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">02 &mdash; Beauty Brand</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Example: Beauty brand on TikTok</h2>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 mb-6">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">5</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">TikTok accounts</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">30</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Active creators</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">200</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Videos/month</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">2.4M</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Monthly views</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Campaign structure</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A DTC skincare brand launched five TikTok accounts, each niched around a specific content angle: "acne solutions for adults," "skincare routines under $30," "dermatologist-approved products," "morning vs. evening routines," and "ingredient breakdowns." Each account was warmed up for two weeks with niche engagement before the first creator video went live.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Thirty creators were onboarded across the five accounts &mdash; six per account. Each creator produced 6&ndash;8 videos per month. The content mix included GRWM (get ready with me) routines, before-and-after sequences, product comparisons, and "dermatologist reacts" commentary-style videos. Creators were paid $3.50 CPM with a $15 base per video.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What worked</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>GRWM morning routines</strong> consistently outperformed other formats, averaging 3x the views of product-only content</li>
          <li><strong>Before-and-after content</strong> with a 4-week timeline drove the highest save rates (8&ndash;12% save rate vs. 2% average)</li>
          <li><strong>Ingredient breakdowns</strong> ("why niacinamide works") attracted a different audience segment and cross-pollinated followers across accounts</li>
          <li><strong>Top 3 organic posts</strong> were promoted as <a href="/spark-ads/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Spark Ads</a>, generating $0.08 CPC &mdash; roughly 60% cheaper than cold creative</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Key takeaway</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Niching each account tightly was critical. The "acne solutions" account reached 500K monthly views by month two because the algorithm had a clear audience to target. The broader "skincare routines under $30" account took longer to gain traction because the niche was wider.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 Tech/SaaS -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="tech">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">03 &mdash; Tech/SaaS</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Example: Tech/SaaS product demos on Instagram</h2>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 mb-6">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">3</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Instagram accounts</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">15</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Active creators</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">90</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Reels/month</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">1.1M</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Monthly views</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Campaign structure</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A project management SaaS tool ran three Instagram accounts: "productivity hacks for freelancers," "remote team workflows," and "startup tools under $50/month." Fifteen creators (five per account) produced screen-recorded Reels showing the product solving real problems &mdash; organizing client projects, automating task assignments, building dashboards.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The content was intentionally low-production. Creators used screen recordings with voiceover, sometimes filmed at their desks with the product open on screen. The format that worked was "I replaced [expensive tool] with this" and "How I manage 12 clients with one app." Creators earned $4 CPM with no base payment.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What worked</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>"Tool replacement" Reels</strong> ("I replaced Notion + Trello + Slack with this one app") drove the highest engagement &mdash; average 45K views vs. 8K for standard demos</li>
          <li><strong>Problem-first hooks</strong> outperformed feature-first hooks by 4x. "I was losing 3 hours a day to status meetings" vs. "This app has a built-in Kanban board"</li>
          <li><strong>Carousel-style Reels</strong> (text overlay showing a workflow step by step) earned the highest save rates</li>
          <li>Top performers were converted to <a href="/partnership-ads/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Partnership Ads</a> on Meta, driving $2.40 cost per trial signup</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Key takeaway</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">SaaS canvas UGC works best when creators demonstrate the product solving a pain point the viewer has right now. Abstract feature demos do not perform. The content needs to feel like a recommendation from a peer, not a product walkthrough.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 Food/CPG -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="food">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">04 &mdash; Food/CPG</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Example: Food/CPG brand recipe content</h2>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 mb-6">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">4</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">TikTok + IG accounts</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">20</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Active creators</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">120</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Videos/month</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">3.8M</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Monthly views</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Campaign structure</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A plant-based protein brand ran four accounts across TikTok and Instagram: "high-protein meal prep," "gym snacks that taste good," "5-minute protein recipes," and a Spanish-language account targeting the Latin American fitness market. Twenty creators produced recipe-style content featuring the brand's products as ingredients in meals and snacks.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Each creator filmed in their own kitchen. Content ranged from 15-second quick recipe clips to 60-second "full meal in a minute" formats. The brand shipped products monthly to creators and paid $3 CPM plus $10 per video base. The Spanish-language account was managed separately with five local creators.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What worked</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>ASMR cooking clips</strong> (close-up shots, crunching sounds, no voiceover) consistently went viral &mdash; three posts crossed 1M views in the first two months</li>
          <li><strong>"What I eat in a day" format</strong> earned 2.5x the average engagement when the creator showed all three meals with the product appearing naturally</li>
          <li><strong>The Spanish-language account</strong> reached 800K monthly views with just five creators, demonstrating the value of geo-niching in underserved markets</li>
          <li><strong>Recipe videos with a clear calorie/protein count on screen</strong> drove 6x the save rate compared to videos without nutritional info</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Key takeaway</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Food content has a natural advantage in canvas UGC: it is inherently shareable, highly rewatchable, and the product integration feels organic. The multi-language account strategy opened a new audience segment with minimal incremental cost.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 Fitness -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="fitness">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">05 &mdash; Fitness App</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Example: Fitness app transformation stories</h2>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 mb-6">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">6</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Accounts (TT + IG + YT)</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">25</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Active creators</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">150</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Videos/month</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">4.2M</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Monthly views</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Campaign structure</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A fitness tracking app ran six accounts across TikTok, Instagram, and YouTube Shorts. The niches were "home workouts for beginners," "gym fails and fixes," "30-day challenge results," "workout routines for busy parents," "weightlifting form checks," and "running for non-runners." Twenty-five creators were split across accounts based on their fitness background and content style.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The standout format was transformation content. Creators documented their progress over 30&ndash;90 days using the app, posting weekly check-ins on the brand accounts. This created narrative arcs that followers could follow over time &mdash; a powerful retention mechanism. Creators earned $5 CPM, reflecting the higher CPA value in the fitness app category.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What worked</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>30-day transformation series</strong> drove follower growth 5x faster than standalone workout videos because viewers followed the account to see progress updates</li>
          <li><strong>"Gym fail" content</strong> (common form mistakes, then the correct form with the app's guidance) had the highest share rate of any format</li>
          <li><strong>The "running for non-runners" account</strong> was the top performer &mdash; relatable beginner content attracted a massive underserved audience</li>
          <li><strong>YouTube Shorts repurposing</strong> of top TikTok performers generated an additional 30% reach with zero additional production cost</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Key takeaway</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Serial content (multi-part transformation stories) creates a reason to follow the brand account, which is rare in short-form video. This approach converts one-time viewers into repeat followers, compounding the value of the canvas over time.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 06 Fashion -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="fashion">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">06 &mdash; Fashion</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Example: Fashion try-on hauls</h2>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 mb-6">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">3</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">TikTok accounts</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">18</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Active creators</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">100</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Videos/month</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-[20px] font-bold text-accent">2.8M</div>
              <div class="text-[12px] text-muted">Monthly views</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Campaign structure</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A mid-range fashion e-commerce brand ran three TikTok accounts: "work outfits under $100," "date night looks," and "capsule wardrobe basics." Eighteen creators with diverse body types and personal styles produced try-on haul content, outfit-of-the-day (OOTD) videos, and "style this one piece five ways" format content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The brand shipped seasonal collections to creators monthly. Each creator filmed 5&ndash;6 videos per shipment, styling the same pieces differently. This gave the accounts a cohesive product focus while keeping the content varied. Creators earned $3 CPM plus they kept the clothing &mdash; an effective non-cash incentive in the fashion vertical.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What worked</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>"Style this one piece 5 ways"</strong> was the top format &mdash; high save rate (9%) because viewers bookmarked it as outfit inspiration</li>
          <li><strong>Outfit transition videos</strong> (quick cuts between casual and dressed-up versions) performed 3x average in the first 6 seconds of watch time (<a href="/hook-rate/" class="text-accent hover:underline">hook rate</a>)</li>
          <li><strong>Diverse body representation</strong> across creators significantly widened the audience. Comments frequently referenced seeing "someone who looks like me" wearing the clothes</li>
          <li><strong>Seasonal trend content</strong> ("fall wardrobe essentials 2026") tapped into seasonal search spikes and drove organic discovery</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Key takeaway</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Fashion canvas UGC benefits enormously from creator diversity. Unlike influencer campaigns where you pick one aspirational figure, canvas programs can run multiple creators with different styles, body types, and aesthetics on the same brand account. This broadens appeal without diluting the account's niche.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07 Content Formats -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="formats">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Formats</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Content formats that perform best</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Across all five examples above, certain content formats consistently outperformed others. These are the formats that generate disproportionate views and engagement in canvas UGC programs.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Format</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Why it works</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Best for</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">GRWM / Routine</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Viewers watch the full sequence; high retention</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Beauty, skincare, fitness</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Before &amp; after</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Curiosity gap keeps viewers engaged</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Fitness, skincare, home</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Tool replacement</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Taps into existing frustration with competitors</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">SaaS, tech, productivity</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Recipe / Tutorial</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">High save rate &mdash; viewers bookmark for later</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Food, CPG, cooking</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Transformation series</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Builds narrative arc; drives follow-for-updates</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Fitness, education, apps</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Try-on / Style</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Visual, shareable, outfit-inspiration saves</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Fashion, accessories</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Problem &rarr; Solution</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Strong hook, clear value prop in &lt;3 seconds</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">All verticals</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The common thread: formats that create a reason to watch until the end (curiosity, narrative, visual payoff) outperform formats that front-load the conclusion. This is because platform algorithms reward watch time and completion rate, both of which are signals for organic distribution.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Briefing -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="briefing">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Briefing</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How to brief creators for canvas content</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Canvas UGC briefs are different from traditional UGC briefs. You are not buying a finished deliverable &mdash; you are giving creators a framework to produce content at volume on your brand's account. The brief needs to be tight enough for brand consistency but loose enough for creator authenticity.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What every canvas brief should include</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Account niche and tone.</strong> What the account is about and how it should feel. "Productivity tips for freelancers &mdash; conversational, no jargon, like advice from a friend who figured it out."</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Approved content formats.</strong> List the 3&ndash;5 formats you want creators to use (e.g., GRWM, before/after, tool replacement). Include examples of each format that have worked on the account or competitor accounts.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Hook bank.</strong> Provide 10&ndash;20 proven hooks that creators can adapt. "I replaced [X] with [product] and here's what happened." "Nobody talks about this [niche] hack."</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Product dos and don'ts.</strong> How the product should appear (naturally integrated, not the star). What claims cannot be made. Required disclosures.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Posting cadence.</strong> How many videos per week, preferred posting times, platform-specific requirements.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Performance benchmarks.</strong> What "good" looks like on this account. Share average view counts so creators know the baseline they should aim to beat.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Common briefing mistakes</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>Over-scripting.</strong> Canvas UGC dies when creators read scripts. Give frameworks, not word-for-word copy</li>
          <li><strong>No examples.</strong> Creators learn by seeing. Include links to 5&ndash;10 reference videos that demonstrate the style you want</li>
          <li><strong>Ignoring the account's existing content.</strong> New creators should watch the last 20 posts on the account before filming so their content fits the established tone</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 Metrics -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="metrics">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Metrics</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Metrics to track</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Canvas UGC programs generate a large volume of data. Not all metrics matter equally. Here are the ones that actually drive decisions.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Metric</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">What it tells you</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Good benchmark</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink"><a href="/hook-rate/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Hook rate</a></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">% of viewers who watch past 3 seconds</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">&gt;40%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Avg. watch time</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">How long viewers stay; drives algorithm distribution</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">&gt;50% of video length</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Save rate</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Content value signal; strongest engagement metric</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">&gt;2%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Views per post</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Account health and content-market fit</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Trending up month over month</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Creator hit rate</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">% of a creator's posts that exceed account average</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">&gt;25%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Cost per view</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Total program cost / total views generated</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">$0.003&ndash;$0.006</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Organic-to-paid ratio</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">What % of top organic posts graduate to paid ads</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Top 5&ndash;10% of posts</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Metrics that don't matter (as much as you think)</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>Follower count.</strong> It correlates with reach over time but is a lagging indicator. Focus on views per post and content quality first.</li>
          <li><strong>Like count.</strong> Likes are a weak signal compared to saves, shares, and comments. A post with low likes but high saves is more valuable than the reverse.</li>
          <li><strong>Individual post virality.</strong> One viral post is not a strategy. Track aggregate monthly views and the trend line, not individual post performance.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

